  "text": "Running Heads and Page Numbers\n\nParagraph 1 of the running example. Reading aloud helps students who find print hard to follow. A reader that highlights each word as it is spoken keeps the eyes and ears together, and a reader that remembers where the student stopped makes long assignments manageable.\n\nParagraph 2 of the running example. The loader rebuilds structure from the page: it finds the body text size, treats larger or bolder short lines as headings, joins wrapped lines into paragraphs, and removes the hyphens that typesetting adds at line ends.\n\nParagraph 3 of the running example. Columns are read one after another, top to bottom, so a sentence is never interrupted by the text beside it. Running headers and page numbers repeat on every page and are left out of the reading.\n\nParagraph 4 of the running example. Reading aloud helps students who find print hard to follow. A reader that highlights each word as it is spoken keeps the eyes and ears together, and a reader that remembers where the student stopped makes long assignments manageable.\n\nParagraph 5 of the running example. The loader rebuilds structure from the page: it finds the body text size, treats larger or bolder short lines as headings, joins wrapped lines into paragraphs, and removes the hyphens that typesetting adds at line ends.\n\nParagraph 6 of the running example. Columns are read one after another, top to bottom, so a sentence is never interrupted by the text beside it. Running headers and page numbers repeat on every page and are left out of the reading.\n\nParagraph 7 of the running example. Reading aloud helps students who find print hard to follow. A reader that highlights each word as it is spoken keeps the eyes and ears together, and a reader that remembers where the student stopped makes long assignments manageable.\n\nParagraph 8 of the running example. The loader rebuilds structure from the page: it finds the body text size, treats larger or bolder short lines as